WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.816
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.724 --> 00:00:04.470
PAUL REGINATO:
I'm Paul Reginato,

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.250
a biological engineer at the
McGovern Institute at MIT.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.420
I make maps of cells.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.350
Think of a cell as a tiny
city with all sorts of objects

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.020
interacting in organized ways.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.600
The way most scientists
study cells gives us

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.610
a list of the
objects within them.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.250
That helps, but we need
to know where things are

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.260
in addition to what things are.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.490
That's where I come in.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.060
I make maps of a cell's
DNA, or its genome.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.910
Each cell contains
six feet of DNA.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.330
And all this DNA has to fit
into a tiny space only 10

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.980
microns wide.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.690
Further, it needs
to be organized

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.850
into specific shapes
in different cells

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.560
to function properly.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.630
We developed a
technology that lets

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.110
us read the letters of the
genome right inside the cell.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.060
This allows us to create a map.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.360
By showing us how DNA is
organized inside cells,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.390
our mapping technology will
be useful for understanding

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.600
how neurons process information
and much, much more.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

